# TODO - Versioned host-capability call ABI

## Objective

Generalize the semantic capability-ID pattern proven by the DOOM corpus into the
VM ABI. Keep capability identity separate from transport: define versioning,
argument/result types, pointer/range validation, blocking rules, failure
semantics, capability discovery, and a deterministic call-frame representation
without requiring a literal new Malbolge opcode. Interpreters, JITs, and AOT
runners may lower the same call frame differently, but guest-visible behavior
and
validation must agree. The DOOM source ABI currently exercises version-1
external-effect categories spanning guest-memory provisioning, diagnostics/exit,
video/input, PCM audio, raw file I/O, UDP transport, and monotonic time; those
application-local capabilities are design evidence, not yet the generic VM ABI.

## Acceptance

- One versioned semantic capability registry and deterministic call-frame format
  define capability identity independently from transport/backend selection.
- Argument/result widths, byte order, guest pointers/ranges, discovery,
  blocking/partial-progress behavior, failures, and unsupported-version behavior
  are explicit and fail closed.
- Guest ranges are validated before host effects, and no host pointer, host libc
  calling convention, platform handle, or multimedia ABI leaks into guest state.
- Independent interpreter-, JIT-, and AOT-style semantic transport fixtures
  pass the same ordered capability vectors with equivalent guest-visible memory,
  results, diagnostics, and effect ordering. Production generated-artifact
  runners consume this unchanged ABI under the downstream runner contract.
- The lowering contract does not require a new Malbolge opcode; any eventual
  encoding mechanism remains separate from semantic capability identity.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `runtime/`, `execution/`, `vm/`,
  `tools/tidy/`, and `tests/vm/`.
- Required evidence: canonical frame vectors, accepted/rejected range fixtures,
  discovery/version failures, host-error vectors, blocking/partial-progress
  fixtures, and cross-tier differential results.
- Implemented foundation evidence: independent safe-Rust and pure-C version-one
  codecs use the same 72-byte frame, 16-byte descriptor, and 16-byte
  payload-relative span vectors, with fail-closed registry, range, status,
  response-identity validation, and atomic staged-result publication.
- Implemented schema evidence: `0x0400` defines monotonic nanosecond
  observation, `0x0401` defines relative-duration sleep, `0x0600` defines
  optional canonical UTF-8 execution telemetry, and `0x0601` defines
  guest-directed relative-mouse capture. All are version 1, operation 0, with
  independent safe-Rust and pure-C conformance vectors.
- Implemented dispatch evidence: a safe-Rust outbound transport port and
  application dispatcher validate requests before effects, expose immutable
  request bytes only, validate staged responses before atomic guest-memory
  publication, and keep transport failures distinct from ABI failures.
- Implemented registry evidence: one canonical built-in registry orders
  monotonic time `0x0400`, sleep `0x0401`, telemetry `0x0600`, then relative
  mouse `0x0601`; typed availability never changes semantic identity, behavior
  flags, or ordering. Rust and independent C constructors encode the same exact
  64-byte registry vector; C rejects invalid availability/extent before writes.
  Schema-aware dispatch rejects unknown IDs before effects and malformed clock
  results before guest-memory publication.
- Implemented timing evidence: monotonic time returns exactly one little-endian
  `u64` nanosecond observation; sleep consumes one little-endian `u64` relative
  duration, declares `MAY_BLOCK`, and admits `WOULD_BLOCK` only for a
  `NONBLOCKING` request. Rust and independent C vectors agree.
- Implemented transport evidence: a standard-library safe-Rust timing transport
  uses one private monotonic origin, never exposes wall-clock identity, maps
  clock failures to `HOST_ERROR`, completes zero sleeps, performs positive
  blocking sleeps, and returns `WOULD_BLOCK` for positive nonblocking sleeps.
- Implemented tier-neutral evidence: three distinct transport strategies use
  direct semantic frames, canonical wire round trips, and field reconstruction;
  two-call vectors agree on responses, guest memory, and effect order.
- Downstream ownership evidence: generated-artifact capability lowering and the
  six-platform production runner matrix are explicitly owned by
  `cross-platform-native-capability-runners`, which depends on this ABI and must
  reuse its identities and vectors unchanged.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `deterministic-c-to-malbolge-abi` and
  `canonical-malbolge-target-profile`.

**DOOM execution telemetry extension.**

The two requested extension schemas are now specified and executable at the
contract layer. Telemetry carries a language identifier, source identity,
execution location, and active instruction text; C line reporting and Malbolge
cell/instruction reporting use the same semantic record without influencing
guest
behavior. Monotonic-time and sleep schemas add deterministic wire shapes for
external timing effects. Production generated-artifact lowering and platform
runner integration continue under the downstream runner TODO rather than being
duplicated as a prerequisite of this semantic ABI.

